WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
The Game Genie Era: Finding Value When Anyone Can Spawn an App

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.500
Remember the Game Genie? You plugged it into your NES,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.000
typed in a cryptic hex code, and suddenly you had infinite lives,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.500
a moon jump, and the spread gun.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
That is exactly what Generative AI just did to software development.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.000
With tools like v0, Cursor, and Lovable, anyone with a pulse

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.500
and a keyboard can prompt a full-stack Vue and Express app

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.000
into existence before their morning coffee gets cold.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
So, here is the existential crisis of the week:

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.000
If everyone is a "builder" now, what exactly is a Product Manager supposed to do?

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.000
If the barrier to shipping code has dropped to zero, are we just highly paid cheerleaders?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Short answer: No.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.000
But if your entire job was just acting as a human router—

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
taking business requests, formatting them into Jira tickets,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
and nagging frontend devs about padding—

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.500
you are officially an NPC in this new meta.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Here is how you level up and find meaning when execution is completely commoditized.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000

Writing is Thinking (Stop Outsourcing Your Brain)

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.500
There is a massive temptation to just ask an LLM to "write a PRD for a new user onboarding flow."

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.500
Product legend Marty Cagan recently pointed out the fatal flaw here: writing a PRD is the definition of thinking.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
If you outsource the narrative, you outsource your product sense.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.000
AI is here to augment your intelligence, not replace it.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.000
Use AI to challenge your assumptions, find edge cases in your logic, or summarize user research,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
but do not let it define the core problem.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.500
The moment you stop thinking critically about why you are building something, your value drops to zero.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.500
2. Code is a Commodity; Product Sense is Premium

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Kris Gale famously said, "The value is in what gets used, not in what gets built."

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.000
In the past, 90% of a company's effort was spent just wrestling with the compiler

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
to get the software to exist.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.000
Now that generating the software is cheap, the penalty for building the wrong thing

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
isn't necessarily lost dev hours—it's lost user trust and flooded markets.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Your job is no longer to manage the pipeline of output;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
your job is to relentlessly filter the noise and apply world-class Product Sense.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.000
You have to "fall in love with the problem, not the solution."

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.000
Anyone can spawn a shiny new app, but very few know if it actually solves a bleeding-neck problem for the user.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.500
3. You Are Now the Editor-in-Chief (and the QA Boss)

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
When everyone can generate an app, the differentiator becomes quality and reliability.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.000
LLMs hallucinate. APIs break. Data models drift.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.500
As an AI PM, your new superpower is Evaluation.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.500
You need to understand the unit economics of a feature,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.000
where the model is likely to fail, and how to design graceful fallbacks for the user.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.500
You aren't necessarily writing the Python scripts, but you own the trade-offs.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.500
You are the one building the evaluation sets—

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.000
the 20 weird edge cases that prove the AI isn't going to accidentally leak a customer's data

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.000
or give them insanely wrong advice.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
4. Empathy Doesn't Have an API

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.000
An LLM can write a flawless sorting algorithm for an Express backend,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.500
but it cannot sit in a room with a frustrated user,

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.000
watch their shoulders slump when a dashboard fails to load,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
and understand their emotional state.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.500
That human context is the ultimate moat.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
We are in the golden era of product management. We finally get to stop managing the factory

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.500
and start managing the product.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Grab a controller, player one.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.500
TL;DR

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
When AI makes building apps practically free, code becomes a commodity

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.000
and Product Sense becomes the premium.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.000
Stop acting as a human Jira router and start acting as an Editor-in-Chief.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.000
Don't use AI to do your thinking; use it to test your logic.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.000
The real value is in knowing what to build, setting up strict evaluations,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.500
and intimately understanding the user's pain—

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.500
because empathy doesn't have an API.